{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Patel from the surgery team. I understand you came in with nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and pain around your G-tube site. Can you tell me what’s been going on today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The diarrhea started a few days ago, and then last night I started throwing up. The pain is right at the tube site—like it’s irritated or pulling."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "How many times have you vomited since last night, and are you able to keep any fluids down?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:12:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A bunch of times overnight. Today I can barely sip water without feeling sick."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:13:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Any blood in the vomit or in your stool? Any black, tarry stools?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:13:55",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No blood. Just watery diarrhea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:14:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Any fevers or chills?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:15:05",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No fever that I know of."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see in your history you’ve had a Roux-en-Y gastric bypass with multiple G-tube procedures and a bypass reversal. When did this current pain around the tube start?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:16:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It got worse after the tube was changed and the balloon was made bigger. It’s been sore since then, but now it’s really tender."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:17:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "And tube feeds—have you been using them recently?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I restarted them last weekend, but I stopped them a couple nights ago because the pain got worse. The diarrhea started after I restarted the feeds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s helpful. Right now your vitals look stable. Our plan is bowel rest for the moment, IV fluids to treat dehydration, and anti-nausea medication. We’ll also monitor your electrolytes and urine output closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:19:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Do you think something is wrong with the tube? Like it moved?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:20:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "On exam your abdomen is soft and not distended, and the tenderness is localized at the tube site without signs of peritonitis. That’s reassuring. We’ll keep checking the site and your symptoms. If anything changes—worsening pain, fever, or new concerning signs—we’d escalate evaluation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:21:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m scared because I keep ending up back here. I can’t tell what’s normal anymore."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:22:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I hear you. With your surgical history, even common stomach bugs or feed intolerance can hit harder. Our goal is to get you comfortable, hydrated, and back to tolerating food safely—and to make sure we’re not missing anything dangerous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:23:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What are you giving me for nausea? The pills at home didn’t touch it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:23:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll start with ondansetron, and because you’re vomiting we can give it in a way that doesn’t rely on you keeping pills down. Once the nausea settles, we’ll switch back to oral as tolerated."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:24:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And pain? It’s really sharp at the tube site."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2174-11-30 20:25:10",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Initially we can use IV pain medicine to get you through the worst of it, then transition to oral oxycodone once you’re tolerating a diet. We’ll also avoid NSAIDs and aspirin because of your allergy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2174-11-30 21:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My blood pressure always runs low. Is that going to be a problem with the pain meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2174-11-30 21:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It can be, so we’ll use the lowest effective dose and keep a close eye on your blood pressure. IV fluids should also help support it while you’re not taking much by mouth."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2174-12-01 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How was the night—any more vomiting? How many episodes of diarrhea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2174-12-01 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I threw up once early, but it’s less. Diarrhea is still happening, but maybe not as constant."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2174-12-01 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a step in the right direction. Your labs are reassuring—kidney function looks okay, electrolytes are stable, and your lactate is normal, which suggests you’re not in shock or severe infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2174-12-01 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So you don’t think it’s an infection?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2174-12-01 08:18:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Right now there’s no fever, no concerning exam findings, and no lab signs pointing strongly to infection. We’ll keep monitoring closely, but at this point supportive care is the main treatment."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2174-12-01 08:19:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Can I start eating? I’m starving but scared it’ll come right back up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2174-12-01 08:20:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll start slowly. Today we’ll keep it gentle—small sips and then clear liquids if you’re not vomiting. If you tolerate that, we’ll advance your diet step by step."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2174-12-01 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my reflux meds? I restarted them a couple days before I came in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2174-12-01 08:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re continuing acid suppression here—pantoprazole—and also sucralfate to protect the lining, given your history of ulcer issues. That can help with upper GI irritation while you recover."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2174-12-01 13:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My anxiety is through the roof. I feel trapped here and I keep thinking something bad is happening."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2174-12-01 13:11:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thank you for telling me. We can ask Psychiatry to see you to help with anxiety management while you’re inpatient, especially given your history of depression and PTSD."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2174-12-01 13:12:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Are you going to take away my lorazepam? I take it regularly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2174-12-01 13:13:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re not trying to abruptly stop it. We’ll continue your home regimen as appropriate and coordinate with Psychiatry. We’ll also be careful because sedating meds can add up with pain medicine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2174-12-02 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning. How did you do with liquids overnight?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2174-12-02 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I kept down broth and some water. No vomiting overnight."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2174-12-02 08:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. Today we can advance your diet. Our usual approach is gradual, and if you keep doing well we can move toward a regular diet."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2174-12-02 08:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the tube feeds? I’m afraid to restart them because that’s when the diarrhea started."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2174-12-02 08:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Given the timing, we’re holding tube feeds for now while your gut settles. We’ll focus on oral intake as tolerated and track your symptoms. If diarrhea worsens with any change, we’ll reassess."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2174-12-02 12:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Psychiatry came by. They mentioned a clonidine patch. What is that for?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2174-12-02 12:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Clonidine can sometimes help with anxiety symptoms and physical hyperarousal. The concern for you is it can lower blood pressure, and you’ve had intermittent low systolic readings here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2174-12-02 12:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So you’re not going to do it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2174-12-02 12:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re not planning to send you home on it because of the low blood pressure risk. It’s something you can discuss with your outpatient psychiatrist, who knows your longer-term history and can monitor you closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2174-12-03 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "How’s your abdominal pain today, especially around the G-tube site?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2174-12-03 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Still tender, but not as intense. I can move around without it spiking as much."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2174-12-03 09:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. Since you’re tolerating food better, we’ll transition away from IV pain medication and use oral oxycodone as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2174-12-03 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m worried about taking opioids. Will I get constipated or dependent?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2174-12-03 09:09:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Those are real concerns. We’ll use the smallest amount for the shortest time, only as needed. Constipation can happen, so we’ll keep an eye on your bowel pattern—especially since you’ve also had diarrhea. The goal is to taper off as your pain improves."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2174-12-03 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I ate some regular food at lunch. I’m nervous it’ll trigger vomiting again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2174-12-03 14:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s understandable. If you can keep it down without worsening pain or vomiting, that’s a good sign. Keep portions small and go slowly. Let us know right away if nausea ramps up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2174-12-04 08:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning. Any vomiting in the last 24 hours? How’s the diarrhea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2174-12-04 08:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No vomiting. Diarrhea is much better—still loose sometimes, but not constant."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2174-12-04 08:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Excellent. You’re tolerating a regular diet, and your hydration looks better. We’ll keep encouraging walking and using the incentive spirometer to keep your lungs strong while you’re in bed part of the day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2174-12-04 08:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Why do I need the leg squeezers and shots? I’m not having surgery."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2174-12-04 08:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Even without surgery, being in the hospital and less active increases the risk of blood clots. The compression boots and the heparin shots lower that risk, along with getting up and walking."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2174-12-04 13:00:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My blood pressure dipped again earlier. Is that dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2174-12-04 13:01:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve seen some low systolic readings in the 80s to 90s at times, but you’ve been alert and not showing signs of poor perfusion. We’ll keep monitoring, make sure you’re hydrated, and avoid adding medications that could lower it further."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2174-12-05 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Today looks like a good day to plan for discharge if you continue to do well. How are you feeling this morning?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2174-12-05 09:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better. I’m eating, I’m not throwing up, and the pain is manageable. I’m just worried it’ll come back when I’m home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2174-12-05 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That worry makes sense. The key things at home are: keep up with fluids, eat small frequent meals, and use ondansetron as needed for nausea. If you start vomiting and can’t keep down fluids or meds, or you feel dehydrated—dizzy, dry mouth, fast heartbeat—come back in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2174-12-05 09:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the G-tube site pain? Do I need antibiotics?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2174-12-05 09:14:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Right now there’s no fever and no signs suggesting an infection during this stay. We’ll have you watch for redness spreading, pus-like drainage, worsening swelling, or fever—those would be reasons to call or come in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2174-12-05 09:15:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Am I going home on the same meds I came in with?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2174-12-05 09:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes—resume your regular home medications. You’ll continue pantoprazole and sucralfate for stomach protection, ranitidine at night, and your psychiatric medications as prescribed. You’ll also have oxycodone for breakthrough pain and ondansetron as needed for nausea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2174-12-05 09:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "How should I take the oxycodone safely?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 68,
      "time": "2174-12-05 09:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take it only as prescribed and only if you need it. Don’t drive or operate machinery while taking it, and avoid mixing it with anything that makes you extra sleepy. If your pain is improving, start spacing doses out and stop as soon as you can."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 69,
      "time": "2174-12-05 09:19:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Do I need to do anything special because of my MRSA history?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 70,
      "time": "2174-12-05 09:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s mainly important for healthcare settings so we can use the right precautions if needed. At home, focus on good hand hygiene and keeping the tube site clean and dry. If you ever develop a skin infection, let your doctor know about the MRSA history."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 71,
      "time": "2174-12-05 14:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Before you go, I want to review return precautions: come back for chest pain, trouble breathing, fever over 101.5, vomiting that prevents fluids or meds, signs of dehydration, blood in vomit or stool, burning with urination or blood in urine, or worsening abdominal pain—especially if it changes location."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 72,
      "time": "2174-12-05 14:51:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. And activity—can I lift things?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 73,
      "time": "2174-12-05 14:52:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take it easy and keep walking several times a day. Avoid heavy lifting until you follow up with your surgeon. Rest, hydrate, and gradually return to normal activity as you feel stronger."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 74,
      "time": "2174-12-05 14:53:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I’m still anxious, but I feel more prepared."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 75,
      "time": "2174-12-05 15:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. You did the right thing coming in. We’ll discharge you home now that you’re tolerating a regular diet, walking independently, and your symptoms have improved."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2174-11-30 19:58:00",
    "admission_end": "2174-12-05 15:06:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with complex bariatric surgical history (prior Roux-en-Y gastric bypass with subsequent gastrostomy tube management and later bypass reversal) was admitted for nausea, vomiting, watery diarrhea, and abdominal pain localized to the G-tube site after restarting tube feeds and then developing poor oral tolerance. She was managed supportively with bowel rest, intravenous fluids, antiemetics, resumption of ulcer/GERD prophylaxis (pantoprazole and sucralfate), and close monitoring of intake/output and labs, without evidence of fever, bleeding, or other signs of infection during hospitalization. Pain control was transitioned from IV morphine to oral oxycodone once she tolerated diet; Psychiatry was consulted for anxiety, and a clonidine patch was considered but not continued due to intermittently low systolic blood pressures. By hospital day 2 her diet was advanced to regular and was well tolerated, symptoms improved, and she was discharged home ambulatory and hemodynamically stable with return precautions and continuation of home medications including PRN ondansetron and short-course oxycodone.",
    "problems": [
      "Nausea with vomiting, unspecified",
      "Gastrostomy status",
      "Major depressive disorder, single episode, unspecified",
      "Diarrhea, unspecified",
      "Unspecified abdominal pain",
      "Bariatric surgery status",
      "Gastro-esophageal reflux disease without esophagitis",
      "Personal history of Methicillin resistant Staphylococcus aureus infection",
      "Post-traumatic stress disorder, unspecified",
      "Obesity, unspecified",
      "Body mass index [BMI] 33.0-33.9, adult"
    ]
  }
}
